# Netlify redirects for the VeriHire client.
#
# These live in `client/public/` rather than in `netlify.toml` on purpose: everything in
# public/ is copied verbatim into dist/, so the rules travel with the build artifact and
# apply identically whether the deploy came from a CI git build or a direct upload of the
# folder. Redirects in netlify.toml are resolved relative to the repo, which makes them
# silently absent from a `--dir` deploy.
#
# ★ Order matters — Netlify takes the FIRST match. The API rules must precede the SPA
# fallback, or /api/v1/jobs would be answered with index.html and a 200, and axios would
# try to parse a web page as JSON.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- API proxy
#
# This has to be a proxy rather than pointing the client at the API's own domain via
# VITE_API_URL. The refresh token is an httpOnly SameSite=Strict cookie, and Strict means
# the browser will not attach it to a cross-site request — so a cross-origin API would
# authenticate on login and then fail every token refresh, logging users out the moment
# the 15-minute access token expired. Proxying keeps the browser on a single origin, which
# is the arrangement the cookie flags and the vite dev proxy were both written for.
#
# The `!` forces the rule even though a file could otherwise shadow it, and the API's own
# prefix is /api/v1, so :splat carries the version through unchanged.
/api/*  https://verihire-api.onrender.com/api/:splat  200!

# If the API is ever taken down, comment the rule above and uncomment this one: it fails in
# the envelope shape the client already parses, rather than letting the SPA fallback answer
# API calls with HTML and a 200 — which surfaces as a TypeError deep inside a component
# instead of an error state.
# /api/*  /api-unavailable.json  503!

# ------------------------------------------------------- missing build assets
#
# ★ This rule must come before the SPA fallback, and leaving it out is a real outage.
#
# Vite fingerprints every chunk, so a deploy changes the hashes. A tab that was open across
# that deploy still holds the old index, and the moment it visits a lazy route it requests
# a chunk name that no longer exists. Without this rule the fallback below answers that
# request with index.html and a 200 — the browser gets text/html where it expected a
# module, refuses to execute it (nosniff makes that certain), the dynamic import rejects,
# React.lazy throws, and the whole app unmounts to a blank page. It looks like the page
# crashed; the page was never loaded.
#
# A 404 is the honest answer for a file that is genuinely gone, and it is what lets the
# error boundary recognise a stale build and offer a reload instead of a blank screen.
# Existing assets are unaffected: Netlify serves a real file before consulting a
# non-forced redirect, so this only ever fires for one that has been deployed away.
/assets/*  /index.html  404

# ------------------------------------------------------------- SPA fallback
#
# Deep links like /jobs/abc123 are client-side routes with no file on disk. 200 rather
# than 301 so the URL the user typed stays the URL in the address bar.
/*  /index.html  200
